    "narrative": "Our strongest offensive catalyst today is **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (74.14), who benefits from a rare combination of an elite batting order spot and an opposing pitcher in Ferguson who is susceptible to stolen bases. With Ferguson\u2019s slow delivery providing a distinct lane for speed, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains our must-start anchor. Following closely is **Bryce Harper** (73.70), whose elite BvP history against Alcantara and placement in the three-hole makes him a high-floor producer despite a slight park penalty. Meanwhile, **Geraldo Perdomo** (69.84) and **Jonathan Aranda** (68.94) round out our top-tier plays, both capitalizing on favorable matchups against Chandler and Quantrill, respectively. The heavy usage of **Geraldo Perdomo** and **Jonathan Aranda** is a direct response to their projected run-scoring environments, allowing us to maximize category accumulation where others might play it safe.\n\nIn terms of bench decisions, **Jung Hoo Lee** (46.14) was the most difficult exclusion from today\u2019s active roster. While he carries a serviceable score, he was squeezed out by the sheer volume of high-ceiling options and a difficult matchup against Henderson; the optimizer favored the platoon-advantaged upside of **Gavin Sheets** (58.63) and **Taylor Ward** (52.46) to push our weekly HR/RBI totals. We are also closely monitoring the potential volatility in the **Luis Arraez** (49.70) start. While he slots into our lineup for his elite average, he faces the toughest matchup of the day in Henderson (2.88 xERA); we are banking on the wind blowing out and his leadoff placement to mitigate the risk posed by such an elite opposing arm. Ensure your game caps remain in focus, as the bench remains anchored by **JJ Bleday** and **Braden Montgomery**, both of whom currently fall below our 40-point efficiency floor.",
